27. DEFINITION OF SUCCESS

The V3 rebuild should not be considered successful merely because:

the application starts,

the board renders,

pieces move,

or a game can be completed.


A successful rebuild must achieve all of the following:

Rules

The implementation consistently follows the current Hess rules.

Interactions

Complex mechanics resolve correctly, including unusual combinations.

State

No legal sequence should permanently freeze the game.

Multiplayer

Clients cannot manipulate authoritative game state or reveal information they should not possess.

UI

Players can understand and interact with Hess without being overwhelmed.

Academy

Players can experiment with pieces and mechanics directly.
AI
Hess AI can legally and meaningfully reason about the game's unique mechanics.
Testing
Known failures become reproducible automated tests.
Extensibility
Future Hess rules can be introduced without rebuilding the entire engine from scratch.
Maintainability
There is one clear source of truth instead of competing implementations.
28. V3 GOLDEN RULE
The most important principle for the rebuild is:
Do not build Hess around what the old code happens to do. Build the code around what Hess is supposed to be.
The existing repository tells us where Hess has been.
The V3 documents define where Hess is going.
The actual games tell us what we may have overlooked.
And playtesting will continue to tell us what we discover next.
Hess remains a living game.
The architecture must be stable enough to make it reliable, but flexible enough to let the game continue becoming something neither the original prototype nor this document can completely predict.
